## Dependency order and evidence policy for integration

Keep definitions and finite, conditional mathematics upstream of QFT applications; build SMEFT/HEFT and supersymmetric operator classes only after the algebraic and power-counting contracts; derive matching/running after these contracts; use analyticity/positivity after the exact amplitude inputs are fixed; place measured EFT/SUSY outcomes as `experiment` items only after operational observables and likelihood/statistical methods are specified. An experiment can be a logical prerequisite only when a later physical theorem explicitly consumes its reported estimate as an empirical premise with conditions and uncertainty carried forward. Ordinary `supported_by`, `tested_by`, and `motivated_by` relationships remain outside the acyclic `deps` graph.

For each proposed proof, identify a precise theorem, proposition, or conditional calculation and give the full local argument. Cite sources for fidelity and context; a citation alone does not discharge proof. For each empirical item, maintain separate blocks for apparatus/measurement procedure, source-reported observations, uncertainty and systematics, theoretical prediction, and statistical inference. Preserve source wording where numerical claims matter. Never infer an observation from a model, reconstruct unavailable weights/covariances, or apply a null-search bound outside its published model family.

The useful minimal mathematical sequence is: (1) formal series, finite-dimensional linear algebra, polynomial modules, Lie/reductive group actions and invariant quotients; (2) test-function/distribution and Fourier tools for local actions and regulated perturbative amplitudes; (3) finite-regulator Gaussian integration for the one existing exact shell model; (4) ODE existence and matrix evolution for explicitly truncated RG systems; (5) complex analysis/residue/dispersion integrals and a spectral/optical-theorem bridge for amplitude positivity; and (6) measurable probability, likelihoods, profile-likelihood ratio and coverage methods for reported inference. Missing bridges must be added as mathematical-domain items with mathematical-only dependencies or treated as open obligations. Physics postulates, model interpretations, detector calibration assumptions, and measured input remain physical/empirical interfaces.

Do not mark an established EFT theorem as an `open-problem` simply because its proof is absent locally. The missing generic Wilsonian integration, operator-quotient proof, threshold matching error bounds, gauge-compatible all-order renormalization, HEFT/SMEFT full correspondence, massless/gravitational positivity extension, and model-independent supersymmetric threshold claims are authoring or scope obligations unless a precise current source/status review shows the mathematical statement is genuinely unresolved. Conversely, do not put a source-backed conjecture, formal perturbative identity, or experimental interpretation into a proved dependency without the actual hypotheses and argument.

