## Statement

Assume AC. Let $d\ge1$, let $C_1,C_2$ be real symmetric positive-semidefinite $d\times d$ covariance matrices, and let $f:\mathbb R^d\to[0,\infty]$ be Borel. With $T_C$ the normalized finite Gaussian shell transform,
$$T_{C_2}(T_{C_1}f)(x)=T_{C_1+C_2}f(x)\qquad(x\in\mathbb R^d),$$
as an equality in $[0,\infty]$. In particular, for every finite nonnegative Borel potential $V$,
$$\exp[-(V_{C_1})_{C_2}(x)]=\exp[-V_{C_1+C_2}(x)].$$
The Gaussian measures are normalized probabilities, so no determinant/vacuum constant appears; changing to unnormalized Gaussian weights requires carrying their normalization factors. This is an exact finite-regulator semigroup identity. The proposition does not assert any equation for the derivative with respect to $t$ of $V_{C(t)}(x)$ along a parameterized covariance $C(t)$; deriving such an equation would require a specified differentiable covariance path and separate differentiability and integrability hypotheses.

